phase1.code:
import java.util.*;

public class ArrayListExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Creating ArrayLists
        System.out.println("=== Creating ===");
        ArrayList<String> names = new ArrayList<>();
        ArrayList<Integer> numbers = new ArrayList<>(Arrays.asList(10, 20, 30));

        // Adding elements
        System.out.println("=== Adding ===");
        names.add("Alice");
        names.add("Bob");
        names.add("Charlie");
        names.add(1, "Diana");  // Insert at index 1
        System.out.println("After adding: " + names);

        // Accessing elements
        System.out.println("=== Accessing ===");
        System.out.println("First name: " + names.get(0));
        System.out.println("Size: " + names.size());

        // Iterating
        System.out.println("=== For-each loop ===");
        for (String name : names) {
            System.out.println("  " + name);
        }

        System.out.println("=== For loop with index ===");
        for (int i = 0; i < names.size(); i++) {
            System.out.println("  Index " + i + ": " + names.get(i));
        }

        // Removing elements
        System.out.println("=== Removing ===");
        names.remove(2);  // Remove by index
        names.remove("Alice");  // Remove by object
        System.out.println("After removing: " + names);

        // Checking contents
        System.out.println("=== Checking ===");
        System.out.println("Contains 'Bob'? " + names.contains("Bob"));
        System.out.println("Index of 'Bob': " + names.indexOf("Bob"));
        System.out.println("Is empty? " + names.isEmpty());

        // ArrayList with different types
        System.out.println("=== Numbers ===");
        numbers.add(40);
        numbers.add(50);
        System.out.println("Numbers: " + numbers);

        // Null handling
        System.out.println("=== Null values ===");
        ArrayList<String> nullable = new ArrayList<>();
        nullable.add(null);
        nullable.add("Hello");
        nullable.add(null);
        System.out.println("With nulls: " + nullable);
        System.out.println("Size: " + nullable.size());

        // ArrayList to array
        System.out.println("=== To Array ===");
        String[] array = names.toArray(new String[0]);
        System.out.println("Array: " + Arrays.toString(array));

        // Clear
        System.out.println("=== Clear ===");
        names.clear();
        System.out.println("After clear: " + names);
        System.out.println("Size: " + names.size());
        System.out.println("Is empty? " + names.isEmpty());
    }
}

phase2.brokenCode:
import java.util.*;

public class Test {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        ArrayList<String> list = new ArrayList<>();
        list.add("A");
        list.add("B");

        // Removing while iterating
        for (String s : list) {
            if (s.equals("A")) {
                list.remove(s);  // ERROR!
            }
        }
        System.out.println(list);
    }
}

phase2.explanation:
[[warn:ConcurrentModificationException]] — you can't remove from a list while a for-each loop is walking it.

Here's why: a for-each loop is secretly using an [[term:Iterator]] under the hood, and that iterator keeps a running count of how many times the list has changed. The instant list.remove() changes that count from underneath it, the next call to next() notices the mismatch and throws rather than silently walking a list that's no longer what it expected.

[[key:the fix is to let the iterator do the removing itself]], since it's allowed to modify the list it owns without tripping its own check:

Iterator<String> it = list.iterator();
while (it.hasNext()) {
    String s = it.next();
    if (s.equals("A")) {
        it.remove();  // safe — the iterator is doing it
    }
}

Or, if you don't need the rest of the loop body, list.removeIf(s -> s.equals("A")) does the same thing in one line.

phase2.fixedCode:
import java.util.*;

public class Test {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        ArrayList<String> list = new ArrayList<>();
        list.add("A");
        list.add("B");

        // Removing safely with the iterator's own remove()
        Iterator<String> it = list.iterator();
        while (it.hasNext()) {
            String s = it.next();
            if (s.equals("A")) {
                it.remove();  // Safe!
            }
        }
        System.out.println(list);
    }
}

